\chapter{From a Canvas API token to FeedbackFruits}
\label{Launch}

This chapter gets us an FBF access token, starting from nothing but the
Canvas API token that [[canvaslms]] already stores.

\section{The problem: FBF trusts only Canvas}

FBF has no login for KTH users.
A user reaches FBF by opening an FBF assignment in Canvas, and the browser
then runs an LTI~1.3 launch, which is an OpenID Connect login where Canvas is
the identity provider.
A browser capture (HAR file) of opening such an assignment shows three hops.
\begin{description}
\item[Hop 1, the Canvas page.] The assignment page contains a hidden form,
  the \emph{tool form}, which the page submits automatically.
  It POSTs [[iss]], [[login_hint]], [[client_id]], [[lti_message_hint]] and a
  few more fields to FBF's [[/v1/lti/login]].
\item[Hop 2, the OIDC login.] FBF answers with redirects back to Canvas's
  [[/api/lti/authorize]].
  Canvas checks that a logged-in user matching [[login_hint]] is present and
  returns a second auto-submitting form, carrying the signed [[id_token]].
\item[Hop 3, the launch.] That form POSTs to FBF's [[/v1/lti/launch]].
  FBF verifies the token and redirects to the activity, setting two cookies:
  [[access_token]], a JWT valid for 30~minutes, and [[refresh_token]], valid
  for 14~days.
  The activity page is FBF's web app, which calls the API at the
  [[api-url]] given in a [[<meta>]] tag.
\end{description}

Nothing in this chain is secret ahead of time: the [[id_token]] exists only
once a logged-in Canvas session asks for it in hop~2.
That is why an earlier attempt failed, which tried to exchange the static
[[code]] in the assignment's launch URL for a token directly.
What we need is a Canvas \emph{session}, not a token to exchange.

\section{The solution: a sessionless launch}

Canvas offers a way to get that session from an API token.
The endpoint
\begin{center}
[[GET /api/v1/courses/:course_id/external_tools/sessionless_launch]]
\end{center}
with [[launch_type=assessment]] and an [[assignment_id]] returns a URL.
For an LTI~1.3 tool, Canvas adds a [[session_token]] parameter to the URL
unless the API token's developer key requires scopes.
(See [[generate_session_token]] in Canvas's
[[lti/concerns/sessionless_launches.rb]].)
Tokens that a user generates in their Canvas settings belong to Canvas's
default developer key, which does not require scopes, so they get one.
The first GET of the URL turns the [[session_token]] into a
[[canvas_session]] cookie, and from then on a [[requests.Session]] can play
the browser's part through all four hops.

Each hop below is a function of its own that checks what it expects to see:
a status, a form with certain fields, a cookie.
When FBF or Canvas changes something, the [[LaunchError]] it raises names the
hop and the expectation that failed, which is where the HAR tools of
\cref{HAR} take over.

