\section{Reading forms and meta tags}

Two hops hand us an HTML page with a form to submit, and the last one a page
whose [[<meta>]] tags name the API.
We need only the [[<form>]], [[<input>]] and [[<meta>]] elements, never the
layout, so the standard library's [[html.parser]] is enough.
It also tolerates the sloppy HTML of real pages, and it spares us the
[[lxml]] dependency.

A [[Form]] holds what a browser would submit: the action URL, the method and
the named inputs with their values.
Inputs outside any form are ignored, as a browser ignores them on submit.

<<imports>>=
import dataclasses
import html.parser
@

<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class Form:
    """An HTML form as a browser would submit it."""

    action: str
    method: str = "get"
    fields: dict = dataclasses.field(default_factory=dict)


class PageParser(html.parser.HTMLParser):
    """Collect the forms and the named meta tags of an HTML page."""

    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.forms = []
        self.meta = {}

    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        attrs = dict(attrs)
        if tag == "form":
            self.forms.append(Form(
                action=attrs.get("action") or "",
                method=(attrs.get("method") or "get").lower()))
        elif tag == "input" and self.forms and attrs.get("name"):
            self.forms[-1].fields[attrs["name"]] = attrs.get("value") or ""
        elif tag == "meta" and attrs.get("name"):
            self.meta[attrs["name"]] = attrs.get("content")
@

The hops call these two helpers rather than the parser.

<<functions>>=
def parse_page(text):
    """Return the `PageParser` holding the forms and meta tags of `text`."""
    parser = PageParser()
    parser.feed(text)
    return parser


def find_form(forms, predicate):
    """Return the first form in `forms` for which `predicate` is true."""
    return next((form for form in forms if predicate(form)), None)
@

Let's verify the parser on a page shaped like Canvas's assignment page: a
hidden tool form with hidden inputs, next to an unrelated search form.
The values are made up.

<<test functions>>=
TOOL_PAGE = """
<form action="/search"><input name="q" value=""></form>
<div class="tool_content_wrapper">
  <form action="https://api.example.com/v1/lti/login" class="hide"
        method="POST" target="tool_content_1" id="tool_form_1">
    <input type="hidden" name="iss" value="https://canvas.instructure.com" />
    <input type="hidden" name="login_hint" value="abc123" />
    <input type="hidden" name="lti_message_hint" value="x.y.z" />
    <button class="btn" type="submit">Load</button>
  </form>
</div>
<meta name="csrf-token" content="t0k3n">
"""

def test_parse_page_finds_tool_form():
  page = parse_page(TOOL_PAGE)
  form = find_form(page.forms, lambda f: f.action.endswith("/lti/login"))
  assert form.method == "post"
  assert form.fields == {"iss": "https://canvas.instructure.com",
                         "login_hint": "abc123",
                         "lti_message_hint": "x.y.z"}
  assert page.meta["csrf-token"] == "t0k3n"

def test_find_form_returns_none_without_match():
  assert find_form(parse_page(TOOL_PAGE).forms,
                   lambda f: "id_token" in f.fields) is None
