\section{Hop 0: the sessionless launch URL}

Asking Canvas for the URL is a plain API call with the Canvas token.
We call it hop~0, since the browser never makes it: it only replaces the
user's click on the assignment.
We check for the [[session_token]] parameter because without it the launch
would fail two hops later with a [[login_required]] error from Canvas,
far from the cause: the developer key of the token requires scopes.

<<imports>>=
import urllib.parse
import requests
@

<<functions>>=
def sessionless_launch_url(canvas_url, canvas_token,
                           course_id, assignment_id, session=None):
    """Return Canvas's sessionless launch URL for an FBF assignment.

    `canvas_url` is the Canvas base URL, e.g. `https://canvas.kth.se`.
    """
    session = session or requests.Session()
    response = session.get(
        f"{canvas_url}/api/v1/courses/{course_id}"
        "/external_tools/sessionless_launch",
        params={"launch_type": "assessment", "assignment_id": assignment_id},
        headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {canvas_token}"})
    if response.status_code != 200:
        raise LaunchError(0, "sessionless launch",
                          f"Canvas answered {response.status_code}")
    url = response.json().get("url")
    if not url:
        raise LaunchError(0, "sessionless launch", "no url in the response")
    if "session_token" not in urllib.parse.parse_qs(
            urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).query):
        raise LaunchError(
            0, "sessionless launch",
            "the URL has no session_token; does the Canvas token's "
            "developer key require scopes?")
    return url
@


\section{Hop 1: the tool form on the Canvas page}

Following the URL logs the session in and lands on the assignment page.
One detail is easy to miss: Canvas answers [[400 endpoint does not support
*/*]] when the request accepts anything, which is what [[requests]] sends by
default.
A browser asks for HTML first, and so must we.
We set the header on the session, so that the redirects after the FBF
hops ask for HTML too.

<<constants>>=
HTML_ACCEPT = ("text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,"
               "*/*;q=0.8")
@

The tool form is the one whose action is FBF's OIDC login endpoint.

<<functions>>=
def open_tool_page(session, launch_url):
    """Follow `launch_url` and return the tool form on the Canvas page."""
    session.headers["Accept"] = HTML_ACCEPT
    response = session.get(launch_url)
    if response.status_code != 200:
        raise LaunchError(1, "Canvas tool page",
                          f"Canvas answered {response.status_code}")
    form = find_form(parse_page(response.text).forms,
                     lambda f: f.action.endswith("/lti/login"))
    if not form:
        raise LaunchError(1, "Canvas tool page",
                          "no form posting to .../lti/login")
    return form
@


\section{Hop 2: the OIDC login}

Submitting the tool form starts the OpenID Connect login.
FBF moves the POST to its regional host with a [[307]], which [[requests]]
follows with the body intact, and then redirects to Canvas's
[[/api/lti/authorize]].
Canvas answers with the form that carries the [[id_token]].

When Canvas refuses, it still answers [[200]] with a form, one that carries
[[error]] and [[error_description]] fields for the tool.
We report those, since they say what Canvas objected to; [[login_required]]
means that the session from hop~1 did not take.

<<functions>>=
def initiate_login(session, tool_form):
    """Submit the tool form; return Canvas's form carrying the `id_token`."""
    response = session.post(tool_form.action, data=tool_form.fields)
    if response.status_code != 200:
        raise LaunchError(2, "OIDC login",
                          f"answered {response.status_code}")
    forms = parse_page(response.text).forms
    refusal = find_form(forms, lambda f: "error" in f.fields)
    if refusal:
        raise LaunchError(2, "OIDC login",
                          f"Canvas refused: {refusal.fields['error']}: "
                          f"{refusal.fields.get('error_description', '')}")
    form = find_form(forms, lambda f: "id_token" in f.fields)
    if not form:
        raise LaunchError(2, "OIDC login", "no form with an id_token")
    return form
@


\section{Hop 3: the launch and the tokens}

Submitting the [[id_token]] completes the launch.
FBF sets its tokens as cookies on its regional domain and redirects to the
activity in its web app.
We return the tokens rather than the cookie jar: the API accepts the access
token as an ordinary [[Authorization: Bearer]] header, so the API client
needs neither the cookies nor the web app's CSRF token.

<<constants>>=
ACCESS_COOKIE = "access_token"
REFRESH_COOKIE = "refresh_token"
@

The cookies live on a domain such as [[.eu.feedbackfruits.com]].
We look them up by name only, so that FBF can move us to another region
without breaking this code.

<<functions>>=
def find_cookie(jar, name):
    """Return the value of the cookie `name` in `jar`, on any domain."""
    return next((cookie.value for cookie in jar if cookie.name == name), None)
@

The redirect target names the FBF course and the activity, both as UUIDs,
for example
[[/courses/lti%2Fcourse/<course>/activities/<activity>]].
They are handy for the caller, who launched exactly this activity.

<<imports>>=
import re
@

<<constants>>=
UUID = r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}"
ACTIVITY_PATH = re.compile(
    rf"/courses/.*?(?P<course>{UUID})/activities/(?P<activity>{UUID})")
<<functions>>=
def activity_from_url(url):
    """Return `(course_uuid, activity_uuid)` from an FBF activity URL."""
    match = ACTIVITY_PATH.search(urllib.parse.unquote(url))
    return (match["course"], match["activity"]) if match else (None, None)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_activity_from_url():
  course = "b4468b1b-0000-4000-8000-000000000001"
  activity = "340a516d-0000-4000-8000-000000000002"
  url = (f"https://eu.example.com/courses/lti%2Fcourse/{course}"
         f"/activities/{activity}?embed=default")
  assert activity_from_url(url) == (course, activity)
  assert activity_from_url("https://eu.example.com/") == (None, None)
@

The page we land on tells us where the API is, in [[<meta name="api-url">]].
Should FBF drop that tag, we fall back on the host that handled the launch
POST, which today is the API host itself.
The API's location is not worth failing the launch over, since we can still
find it.

The tokens are left out of the [[repr]] of an [[FBFLogin]].
A data class shows every field by default, and its [[repr]] turns up where
nobody asked for the tokens: in a failed [[assert]], which [[pytest]]
explains by printing the values involved, in a traceback, in a log line.
Whoever holds the access token is the user for 30~minutes, and with the
refresh token for 14~days.

<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class FBFLogin:
    """The result of a launch: FBF tokens and where to use them."""

    access_token: str = dataclasses.field(repr=False)
    refresh_token: str = dataclasses.field(repr=False)
    api_url: str
    course_uuid: str = None
    activity_uuid: str = None
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_login_repr_hides_the_tokens():
  login = FBFLogin("eyJaccess", "r3fresh", "https://api.example.com")
  shown = repr(login)
  hides_tokens = "eyJaccess" not in shown and "r3fresh" not in shown
  assert hides_tokens
  assert "https://api.example.com" in shown

<<functions>>=
def complete_launch(session, id_token_form):
    """Submit the `id_token` form and return the resulting `FBFLogin`."""
    response = session.post(id_token_form.action, data=id_token_form.fields)
    if response.status_code != 200:
        raise LaunchError(3, "FBF launch",
                          f"answered {response.status_code}")
    access_token = find_cookie(session.cookies, ACCESS_COOKIE)
    if not access_token:
        raise LaunchError(3, "FBF launch", f"no {ACCESS_COOKIE} cookie")
    api_url = parse_page(response.text).meta.get("api-url")
    if not api_url:
        launch_hop = (response.history or [response])[-1]
        parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(launch_hop.url)
        api_url = f"{parts.scheme}://{parts.netloc}"
    return FBFLogin(access_token,
                    find_cookie(session.cookies, REFRESH_COOKIE),
                    api_url.rstrip("/"),
                    *activity_from_url(response.url))
@


\section{The whole launch}

Put together, a launch is hop~0 and the three hops in a row on one session,
which carries the cookies from one hop to the next.
Callers may pass a session of their own, for instance one that records the
traffic.

<<functions>>=
def launch(canvas_url, canvas_token, course_id, assignment_id, session=None):
    """Launch an FBF assignment from Canvas and return an `FBFLogin`.

    `course_id` and `assignment_id` are Canvas IDs of an assignment that
    launches FeedbackFruits.  Raises `LaunchError` naming the hop that failed.
    """
    session = session or requests.Session()
    url = sessionless_launch_url(canvas_url, canvas_token,
                                 course_id, assignment_id, session)
    tool_form = open_tool_page(session, url)
    id_token_form = initiate_login(session, tool_form)
    return complete_launch(session, id_token_form)
@

The hops need Canvas and FBF to run, so the test that covers them replays
a recorded launch (\cref{Testing}); with [[--live]] it launches for real.
The same test body has to pass on both, so it checks what a launch must
deliver whatever the course and the day: tokens, an API on an FBF host
over HTTPS, and the UUIDs of the course and the activity.
In replay the tokens are placeholders, since the recording keeps no secrets,
but they must still be there.
The checks go into variables before the asserts: [[pytest]] prints the
values in a failed assert, and those would include a live token.

<<test functions>>=
import urllib.parse

import pytest
from conftest import ASSIGNMENT_ID, CANVAS_URL, COURSE_ID

@pytest.mark.vcr
def test_launch_gives_tokens_and_where_to_use_them(canvas_token):
  login = launch(CANVAS_URL, canvas_token, COURSE_ID, ASSIGNMENT_ID)
  has_tokens = bool(login.access_token) and bool(login.refresh_token)
  assert has_tokens, "the launch gave no access or refresh token"
  api = urllib.parse.urlsplit(login.api_url)
  assert api.scheme == "https"
  assert api.hostname.endswith(".feedbackfruits.com")
  assert re.fullmatch(UUID, login.course_uuid or "")
  assert re.fullmatch(UUID, login.activity_uuid or "")
@
